export anchor DuplicateExports:
    description:
        There are four ways a name can leave a module. It can be a declaration
        marked export, `export Name`, `from ./X export Name`, or
        `from ./X export *`, which hands on everything X exports. The first
        three are explicit exports. The last is a glob.

        Two `export *` lines can export the same name with two different
        meanings. That is not an error. The module exports every one of them,
        and the compiler changes the output to keep them apart.

        ```piton fragment
        \\\
        from ./cruxible export *
        from ./strataglyph export *
        \\\
        ```

        If both modules export an anchor called Roles, the module does not
        export the plain name Roles, because it cannot mean one of them over
        the other. It exports each one under the name of its source module
        instead: cruxible.Roles and strataglyph.Roles. The name of a source
        module is its file name without .pi, or the name of its directory
        when it is an index.pi. A name that only one glob exports, or that all
        the globs export with the same meaning, stays a plain export.

        The compiled output follows the same names. Here the module compiles
        to an object with the keys cruxible.Roles and strataglyph.Roles, next
        to the keys of its plain exports, in the order of the export lines.

        An import selects an overlapping export by its qualified name. Without
        an alias, the import binds the last part of the name.

        ```piton fragment
        \\\
        from ./index import cruxible.Roles, strataglyph.Roles GlyphRoles
        \\\
        ```

        This brings in Roles from cruxible and GlyphRoles from strataglyph.
        An import of the plain name Roles from that index is an error, because
        the index does not export it. The error names each qualified name that
        the import can use instead. A `from ./index export *` line in another
        module hands on the qualified names as they are.

        An explicit export hides the same name from a glob, and that is not an
        error either. If the module declares or names its own Roles, it exports
        that Roles, and the Roles of each glob stays out.

        Exporting the same thing twice under one name isn't a collision. Two
        `export *` lines that both end up at the same anchor (say, because both
        modules re-export it from a shared file) export one thing, and that's
        fine.

        Only two cases are a compile error, `duplicate-export`. The first is
        two explicit exports of one name with two different meanings. The
        second is two globs that give a name two meanings from source modules
        that have the same name (./a/roles and ./b/roles both qualify it as
        roles.Roles), because the qualified names collide too. The error names
        the clashing name and both modules it comes from. To fix it, export
        one of them under an alias, or replace `export *` with an explicit list
        that leaves one out or renames it.

        ```piton fragment
        \\\
        from ./a export Roles
        from ./b export Roles BRoles
        \\\
        ```

        A duplicate export is still an error, but the compiler keeps one of
        the two so the rest of the program can resolve and every other problem
        gets reported in the same run. A declaration marked export wins
        first, then the other named exports in the order they are written,
        then the `export *` lines in the order they are written. The one that
        isn't kept isn't exported by the module, so if nothing else reaches it,
        `piton reach` reports it as unreachable. The language server reports
        the same error, since it shares the compiler's diagnostics.
